Remove the Password Manager tutorial and the promo that launches it.

The tutorial walks the user from the three-dot menu to Password Manager and
on to installing it as a shortcut. Its second step is:

    TutorialDescription::WaitForAnyOf(
        AppMenuModel::kPasswordAndAutofillMenuItem)
        .Or(AppMenuModel::kPasswordManagerMenuItem),

Both of those app-menu items were removed by
aerium-remove-passwords-autofill-app-menu.patch, so the step waits for two
elements that can never appear. The tutorial opens a help bubble on the app
menu button and then hangs there permanently. Nothing later in it is
reachable either - every remaining step routes through the Password Manager
page that the removed menu items were the way to reach - so there is no
version of this worth repairing step by step.

It is reachable in a normal session. The only launcher is the
kIPHPasswordManagerShortcutFeature tutorial promo, anchored on
kPasswordsOmniboxKeyIconElementId - the key icon that appears in the omnibox
after a password is saved. Aerium removed the autofill and password settings
UI, not the save-password flow, so that icon still appears and the promo can
still offer the tutorial.

Both registrations go, and both lookups they feed are null-safe, which is
what makes removing them the right fix rather than a new crash:

  FeatureRegistry::GetParamsForFeature() returns FindOrNull, documented as
  "Returns the specification for the given feature, or null if not found.
  (Some configurations may be only conditionally registered based on feature
  flags.)" - conditional registration is an expected state, not an error.

  TutorialRegistry::GetTutorialDescription() returns nullptr for an unknown
  id, and GetTutorialIdentifiers() enumerates only registered tutorials, so
  chrome://internals/user-education cannot offer one that is gone.

kPasswordManagerTutorialId and kPasswordManagerTutorialMetricPrefix are
inline constexpr in chrome/browser/user_education/tutorial_identifiers.h, so
dropping the only two uses in this file cannot orphan a constant. Everything
else the removed code referenced is still used here: kLightbulbOutlineIcon by
eight other promos, kPasswordsOmniboxKeyIconElementId by four, and
PasswordManagerUI:: by the share-password promo.

The web_app::WebAppInstallDialogDelegate include becomes unused. It is left
in place deliberately: an unused include is not a diagnostic, while removing
one can break a translation unit that was relying on it transitively, and
that trade is not worth a ten-hour build.

